Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• `trip_start_timestamp`: extract year & month so we can bucket every ride into 2018‑01 … 2018‑12.  
• `company`: the field to group by so we can count rides for each taxi firm.  
• `unique_key`: every row is a single taxi trip, therefore `COUNT(*)` (or `COUNT(unique_key)`) gives the number of trips.  

Typical conditions we will need  
• `EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp)=2018` – keep only 2018 rides.  
• `company IS NOT NULL AND company!=''` – ignore blank company names.  


Ten exploratory → analytical queries
(Every query finishes with `LIMIT 20` as requested.)

```sql
-- 1. Quick look at a handful of 2018 trips
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp)=2018
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. See the distinct company names that appear in 2018
SELECT DISTINCT company
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp)=2018
  AND company IS NOT NULL AND company!=''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Overall trip counts per company for 2018
SELECT company,
       COUNT(*) AS trip_cnt_2018
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp)=2018
  AND company IS NOT NULL AND company!=''
GROUP BY company
ORDER BY trip_cnt_2018 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Number of trips in each month (all companies combined)
SELECT FORMAT_DATE('%Y-%m', DATE(trip_start_timestamp)) AS yr_mo,
       COUNT(*) AS trips
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp)=2018
GROUP BY yr_mo
ORDER BY yr_mo
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Trip counts per company‐month bucket
SELECT company,
       FORMAT_DATE('%Y-%m', DATE(trip_start_timestamp)) AS yr_mo,
       COUNT(*) AS trips
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp)=2018
  AND company IS NOT NULL AND company!=''
GROUP BY company, yr_mo
ORDER BY company, yr_mo
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Same as #5 but include numeric month index to help with window functions
SELECT company,
       EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp)  AS mo,
       COUNT(*) AS trips
FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp)=2018
  AND company IS NOT NULL AND company!=''
GROUP BY company, mo
ORDER BY company, mo
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Use a window function to attach the previous month’s trip count
WITH month_cnt AS (
  SELECT company,
         EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS mo,
         COUNT(*) AS trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp)=2018
    AND company IS NOT NULL AND company!=''
  GROUP BY company, mo
)
SELECT company,
       mo,
       trips,
       LAG(trips) OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY mo) AS prev_mo_trips
FROM month_cnt
ORDER BY company, mo
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Compute month‑to‑month change (increase or decrease)
WITH month_cnt AS (
  SELECT company,
         EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS mo,
         COUNT(*) AS trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp)=2018
    AND company IS NOT NULL AND company!=''
  GROUP BY company, mo
)
SELECT company,
       mo,
       trips,
       trips - LAG(trips) OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY mo) AS diff_from_prev_mo
FROM month_cnt
ORDER BY company, mo
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. For every company pick its largest positive jump between two consecutive months
WITH month_cnt AS (
  SELECT company,
         EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS mo,
         COUNT(*) AS trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp)=2018
    AND company IS NOT NULL AND company!=''
  GROUP BY company, mo
),
diffs AS (
  SELECT company,
         mo,
         trips,
         trips - LAG(trips) OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY mo) AS diff_from_prev_mo
  FROM month_cnt
)
SELECT company,
       MAX(diff_from_prev_mo) AS max_monthly_increase
FROM diffs
GROUP BY company
ORDER BY max_monthly_increase DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER: the three companies with the largest single‑month increase in trip count during 2018
WITH month_cnt AS (
  SELECT company,
         EXTRACT(MONTH FROM trip_start_timestamp) AS mo,
         COUNT(*) AS trips
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.chicago_taxi_trips.taxi_trips`
  WHERE EXTRACT(YEAR FROM trip_start_timestamp)=2018
    AND company IS NOT NULL AND company!=''
  GROUP BY company, mo
),
diffs AS (
  SELECT company,
         mo,
         trips - LAG(trips) OVER (PARTITION BY company ORDER BY mo) AS diff_from_prev_mo
  FROM month_cnt
),
best_jump AS (
  SELECT company,
         MAX(diff_from_prev_mo) AS max_monthly_increase
  FROM diffs
  GROUP BY company
)
SELECT company,
       max_monthly_increase
FROM best_jump
ORDER BY max_monthly_increase DESC
LIMIT 3;      -- we only need the biggest three
```

Run the final query (#10) to obtain the three companies that experienced the highest increase in ride counts between any two consecutive months in 2018.
